paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:165:The calibration router only beats fusion significantly on SeedVL; on GPT-5.4 it trails fusion by 0.5 pp (not significant) and on Qwen3.5 it matches fusion. The oracle leaves 1.3--1.9 pp further headroom, suggesting a VLM-driven router reading image-level features may close more of the gap. Routing is calibrated from 20 items per domain; we evaluate a single expert backbone (DINOv2-giant) and a single frontier non-English VLM (SeedVL); the Route-C component-enumeration prototype (Appendix~\ref{app:routec}) remains at 60\% calibration accuracy on D9 and is not in the reported variant. The failed-variant ablation is GPT-5.4 calibration only. The online expert-signal override threshold ($\rho > 0.8$) is currently calibrated against patch-$k$-NN's normalised score; on industrial domains where SubspaceAD's raw distance is large, the override fires on essentially every \emph{normal}-predicted item and effectively reduces the strategy choice to ``fusion or interpret'' on those domains; a backbone-aware $\rho$ rescaling is left to future work.
