paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:256:\caption{Budget-matched negative controls. \emph{v0 Direct, SubspaceAD fusion, and AnomalyClaw rows are on the test split} (same numbers as Table~\ref{tab:main_results}, repeated for comparison). \emph{``Random escalation'' and ``DINOv2 blend'' rows are calibration-split numbers} — these are disqualified designs we ran only on GPT-5.4 and SeedVL calibration, not on the test splits. Random escalation re-prompts a random 30\% of items; DINOv2 blend swaps SubspaceAD for a weaker patch-NN expert.}
paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:266:\textbf{v0 + 0.2$\cdot$SubspaceAD fusion} & \textbf{1.00} & \textbf{0.835} & 0.813 & \textbf{0.851} \\
paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:278:\caption{VLM$\times$Expert correctness matrix per domain (GPT-5.4 test). Numbers are \% of items. ``Expert-only correct'' is the upper bound of what the agent can recover; ``VLM-only correct'' is what it risks losing by blindly trusting the expert.}
paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:285:Every AnomalyClaw v3 prediction carries a full trace that a human reviewer can audit. For illustration, we show two trace snippets from the actual test runs (Qwen3.5 backbone) — first the autonomous router plan generated for one domain, then a per-item agent execution.
paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:287:\paragraph{Autonomous router plan (D5c BMAD-Liver, Qwen3.5).}
paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:294:\paragraph{Per-item agent execution (D1 Industrial, item \texttt{D1\_0063}, SeedVL).}
paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:296:\textbf{Phase 1 (Plan lookup).} Cached plan for D1 = (\texttt{strategy:fusion}, \texttt{expert:subspacead}, tools \{\texttt{domain\_descriptor}, \texttt{hotspot\_cropper}\}).\\[2pt]
paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:297:\textbf{Phase 2 (Expert).} \texttt{SubspaceAD.score} = 17.95 (global calibration median $m{=}40.0$); \texttt{top-5 patches} at $(22,24), (10,24), (10,22), (33,32), (\ldots)$ in the $48{\times}48$ grid. Derived $\rho = 159.7$, $\kappa = 1.43$.\\[2pt]
paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:298:\textbf{Phase 3 (Online override).} $\hat{y}_{\mathrm{v0}}{=}$normal AND $\rho > 0.8$ $\Rightarrow$ promote strategy \emph{fusion} $\to$ \emph{interpret}.\\[2pt]
paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:303:Every test-split item has an analogous trace, serialised as JSON in the released \texttt{plan} and \texttt{raw\_output} fields. Score fusion, by contrast, produces only a scalar.
paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:310:On D10 calibration (20 items, 4 reference images per query), Route C reaches 60\% accuracy versus v0's 55\%. The gain is modest for two reasons: (i) VLMs struggle to count small similar objects reliably, and (ii) the 20-item calibration split yields noisy threshold estimates. Route C is \emph{not} part of the main-table AnomalyClaw variant reported in Table~\ref{tab:main_results}: the reported numbers use only Routes A/B/D. We include Route C here as a concrete direction for future work and flag it as a prototype rather than a validated component of the agent.
paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:327:\section{Descriptor router rules}
paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:328:\label{app:router_rules}
paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:330:The descriptor router maps each domain family to a default strategy. Rules were frozen once from the benchmark descriptor before any test query; no calibration AUROC was consulted.
paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:334:\caption{Descriptor router rules.}
paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:335:\label{tab:router_rules}
paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:341:industrial / retail / industrial\_visa & fusion & texture-dominant few-shot; expert is reliably discriminative, VLM calibrates \\
paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:342:infrastructure & fusion & concrete cracks flagged by patch expert; descriptor narrows false positives \\
paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:343:logical (D9 MVTec-LOCO) & fusion & component structure visible to patch expert; VLM rules out semantic look-alikes \\
paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:344:dermoscopy (D4) & fusion & patch expert catches localised lesions; VLM rules out imaging artefacts \\
paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:345:medical\_mixed / liver\_ct / brain\_mri & fusion & SubspaceAD complements VLM on non-trivial intensity shifts \\
paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:366:Tools are composed explicitly by each strategy: \emph{direct} uses only the descriptor; \emph{fusion} uses descriptor and expert scores; \emph{interpret} additionally uses the cropper and expert hotspots; \emph{debate} uses the descriptor and optionally the retriever. No tool is invoked outside its strategy's declared contract.
paper/sections/1_introduction.tex:10:    \textbf{(a)} No single strategy wins uniformly across domains: the best direct/fusion/debate/interpret cell differs by family (industrial, medical, logical, GI, change, road). \textbf{(b)} A calibration-driven router matches or beats the strongest single strategy on all three backbones and captures 40--60\% of the oracle gap; a zero-data descriptor router recovers much of the gain for free. \textbf{(c)} Per-domain strategy assignment is well-aligned with the oracle (9--10/12 agreement across backbones).}
paper/sections/1_introduction.tex:14:Two observations drive this work. First, a cross-domain VAD deployment must handle textural industrial defects, semantic road obstacles, brain-MRI lesions, and infrastructure cracks with the same pipeline. The same fixed recipe---no matter how clever---cannot be optimal on all of them simultaneously: we find that on our 12-domain test split, the argmax strategy varies by domain family and gains of up to $+3$ pp macro AUROC are available simply by switching strategy per domain (Figure~\ref{fig:intuition}a). Second, recent ``agentic VAD'' systems~\citep{zhang2025agentiad,chen2026eagle,wei2025autoiad} bundle three decisions---what the VLM attends to, which expert provides evidence, and how to aggregate---into a single monolithic pipeline that is hard to ablate and hard to compare against simpler baselines.
paper/sections/1_introduction.tex:16:We argue that these three decisions should be explicit and decoupled. We introduce \textbf{AnomalyClaw}, a training-free VAD agent organised around three orthogonal axes:
paper/sections/1_introduction.tex:21:    \item \textbf{Strategies} --- inference schemes that combine tools, experts, and VLM calls (direct, fusion, debate, interpret).
paper/sections/1_introduction.tex:24:A lightweight \emph{router} selects a (tools, expert, strategy) triple per image. The router comes in two flavours: \emph{descriptor rules} (one rule per domain family, frozen once from the benchmark descriptor, no labelled data) and \emph{calibration argmax} (per-domain argmax over 20 labelled calibration items). An online expert-signal override can promote any domain to the \emph{interpret} strategy when patch-$k$-NN detects a spatially concentrated anomaly that the VLM missed.
paper/sections/1_introduction.tex:29:    \item We show that \emph{task-anchored domain descriptors} significantly improve all three backbones ($+6.4$/$+4.1$/$+3.2$ pp on GPT-5.4, SeedVL, Qwen3.5-VL-27B; paired bootstrap, $n{=}1418$, all 95\% CIs exclude zero) at zero inference cost; this finding alone changes the conclusions of recent agentic-VAD comparisons (\S\ref{ssec:descriptor_ablation}).
paper/sections/1_introduction.tex:30:    \item We give a controlled study of per-domain strategy variance: argmax strategy varies across domain families, and a calibration-argmax router beats every single-strategy baseline on every backbone (point gains: descriptor router $+1.7$/$+2.2$/$+4.9$ pp over direct on GPT-5.4/SeedVL/Qwen3.5; calibration router $+1.0$/$+3.3$/$+5.6$ pp over direct and $-0.5$/$+1.6$/$+0.1$ pp over fusion---with SeedVL fusion being significantly improved upon, 95\% CI $[+0.02,+3.18]$). An oracle per-domain selector caps achievable gain at $+1.3$/$+2.9$/$+1.5$ pp over fusion.
paper/sections/1_introduction.tex:31:    \item We release \textbf{CrossDomainVAD-11}, a 12-domain-code benchmark (1{,}418 test items) with calibration/dev/test splits, the Tool/Expert/Strategy registry, the router implementation, and stratified paired bootstrap confidence intervals for all headline comparisons.
paper/sections/1_introduction.tex:32:    \item We document six failed agent variants on GPT-5.4 calibration---self-refine, naive debate, gated debate, evidence-injected proposer, third-call arbiter, normal-first---as concrete negative findings that motivate an \emph{adaptive} router rather than an \emph{unconditional} second VLM call.

